1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:09,675
This is Q-GreenFleet, our answer to problem statement 26138: quantum-inspired fuel prediction and green fleet optimisation.

2
00:00:10,725 --> 00:00:17,240
Our India case study has twelve real services, from JNPT to Mundra, all the way to Rotterdam.

3
00:00:17,740 --> 00:00:29,055
Run as fleets run today, it emits about 2.1 million tonnes of CO₂-equivalent a year, and eleven of twelve services fail the 2030 carbon-intensity limit.

4
00:00:30,358 --> 00:00:32,542
First, optimising the whole fleet.

5
00:00:34,546 --> 00:00:41,829
The scenario is India in 2030, with IMO rules, FuelEU and E U carbon trading built in.

6
00:00:42,329 --> 00:00:51,724
Our quantum-inspired optimiser, QMOEA-H, chooses the vessel mix, capacity, speed, fuel and shore power for all twelve routes at once.

7
00:00:52,224 --> 00:00:57,864
Every dot is a complete fleet plan, streaming in live, far below today's plans in grey.

8
00:00:58,364 --> 00:01:06,393
In seconds it evaluates six thousand plans and keeps the 116 best. The recommended plan sits at the knee of the curve.

9
00:01:06,893 --> 00:01:17,162
Compared with today, the recommended plan cuts emissions by 60 percent, fuel by 52 percent and cost by 23 percent, while meeting every constraint.

10
00:01:17,662 --> 00:01:25,137
And it explains itself: which routes switch to LNG or ammonia, where to slow down, and where to plug into shore power.

11
00:01:25,637 --> 00:01:29,976
Here is the fleet on the map, next to the emission profile of every service.

12
00:01:32,703 --> 00:01:39,666
For each service: the vessel class, number of ships, speed, fuel, shore power and the resulting CII rating.

13
00:01:40,166 --> 00:01:47,534
One click builds a decision report for managers, with the plan, its compliance, its robustness and its abatement costs.

14
00:01:49,838 --> 00:01:51,574
Second, staying compliant.

15
00:01:54,143 --> 00:02:02,322
For the chosen plan: every service's CII rating, the FuelEU pool against its tightening limit, and the carbon-trading cost.

16
00:02:03,625 --> 00:02:05,916
Third, predicting fuel for any ship.

17
00:02:07,867 --> 00:02:15,683
Q-PHYS predicts fuel from speed, load, weather and vessel type, combining physics with a quantum-inspired tensor network.

18
00:02:16,183 --> 00:02:22,484
Speed up, and fuel climbs steeply. The model is certified never to predict less fuel at a higher speed.

19
00:02:23,213 --> 00:02:28,384
Rougher seas add fuel, and the ninety percent confidence band scales with the prediction.

20
00:02:30,410 --> 00:02:35,346
Switch the fuel to green ammonia, and the fuel mass and emissions update instantly.

21
00:02:36,309 --> 00:02:44,339
Inside the tensor network, entanglement entropy shows which inputs the model links together, an idea borrowed from quantum physics.

22
00:02:45,651 --> 00:02:48,773
Fourth, planning the transition to 2050.

23
00:02:49,857 --> 00:02:56,755
Each milestone year to 2050 is optimised under that year's rules, prices and fuel availability.

24
00:02:57,256 --> 00:03:02,554
The fuel mix shifts decade by decade as the targets tighten, while emissions keep falling.

25
00:03:04,405 --> 00:03:10,514
The abatement cost curve shows which measures save money, and which cost dollars per tonne of carbon avoided.

26
00:03:11,014 --> 00:03:18,147
An exact mixed-integer program finds the true optimum in about a second. That is our yardstick for every heuristic.

27
00:03:18,647 --> 00:03:27,508
The same problem, written as a QUBO, is solved by our own simulated quantum annealer, and runs unchanged on D-Wave quantum hardware.

28
00:03:28,008 --> 00:03:32,858
The annealed plan lands just 1.52 percent from the exact optimum.

29
00:03:34,169 --> 00:03:35,883
Finally, the benchmarks.

30
00:03:39,357 --> 00:03:48,453
Q-PHYS predicts fuel within 3.3 percent on known ships, ahead of LightGBM, random forests and neural networks on the same data.

31
00:03:48,953 --> 00:03:56,150
Our optimiser ranks first against the classical algorithms, and lands within one to three percent of the exact optimum.

32
00:03:56,650 --> 00:04:02,652
And we show honestly where classical methods still win: on very large, loosely constrained networks.

33
00:04:07,889 --> 00:04:17,177
And what if the Red Sea closes? One click, and the Europe service reroutes around the Cape of Good Hope, adding about four thousand seven hundred nautical miles.

34
00:04:18,744 --> 00:04:23,338
Q-GreenFleet. Predict every tonne, optimise every voyage. Thank you.
